  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that rituximab may be associated with the development of Arthritis, Infectious through a complex network of molecular interactions. The drug is linked to keratin associated protein 3-2, which is involved in Ludwig's angina, a condition that is causally associated with ribosomal protein S23 pseudogene 1 and glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase pseudogene 16. These pseudogenes are implicated in the pathogenesis of Arthritis, Infectious, indicating a potential role for these genetic elements in the disease process. The scores from the two paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanisms by which rituximab influences these pseudogenes remain unclear. The evidence highlights a possible pathway where rituximab may contribute to the development of Arthritis, Infectious through the dysregulation of keratin-associated proteins and their downstream effects on ribosomal and glycolytic pseudogenes."
